title: Mirror substitution and the inPlaceOf part
pid: ba1eceb1-a3e9-4f52-a4d8-88216f3555e3
body: |2-
  Where a node N stands as content of an editable occurrence in a
  content view, the refresh shows instead the mirror of N that the
  perspective's skgrepo list ranks first, never in the diff view
  ('substitute_mirrors' in =server/refresh/visit.rs=;
  =docs/mirrors.org=, "Mirror substitution", says what N's mirrors are
  and how a tie is broken). The mirrors of N are N and the notes joined
  to it by =forks= relationships, followed either way, under the
  skgrepo restriction; a perspective is a skgrepo S (or the root homes,
  one per tree), and its list is S,
  then every other skgrepo in declaration order. In its own skgrepo N
  itself stands, however many of its mirrors are there; of several in
  one skgrepo other than N's, the least pid stands; where a mirror's
  skgrepo is restricted, a chain of forks is cut there. The refresh
  substitutes at every refresh, a member already shown raw included,
  and re-points a member shown in another's place whenever it is not
  the mirror shown, since it decides from the buffer and the graph as
  they are now. Only content and the view's roots substitute: partner
  folder members, chain nodes and ghosts show the raw node.

  The perspective of a top-level tree of the view is, by default, its
  root's home skgrepo, throughout the tree, beneath notes homed
  elsewhere too ('perspective_skgrepo_of_tree_containing' in
  =server/dbs/in_rust_graph/mirrors.rs=): the root homes. A view of a
  foreign note is drawn from the note's skgrepo; the user's mirrors
  stand there only where that skgrepo holds none and the user's are
  declared first, until the view is switched to a skgrepo's
  perspective ("Set perspective"), after which every tree is drawn
  from that skgrepo's, whatever its root's home. Ownership plays no
  part: where the perspective's skgrepo holds no mirror of N, the first
  declared skgrepo holding one decides.

  A root stands for its original: the node its =(inPlaceOf N)=
  names, else itself; and its home is the tree's root home. It shows
  the mirror of the original its perspective's list ranks first,
  marked =(inPlaceOf N)= unless it is the original
  ('substitute_roots' in =server/refresh/visit.rs=). From the root homes
  that is the original itself, since it stands in its own home, so a
  fresh view, and a view switched back, shows the node asked for, and so
  does a search buffer each of its hits. The root is
  re-pointed where it stands, keeping its viewnode ID, so point on it
  stays there both ways; its children follow from its own visit. A
  re-pointed root's containers folder is drawn afresh, as a fresh view
  of the node it now shows would draw it: what contains that node, but
  its view-ancestors, or no folder where nothing does; so switching back
  draws the original's folder again. The
  view keeps its view ID, and the clients keep the buffer's name.

  The buffer whose approved save creates a fork is the post-save exception. A
  root named by one of that save's exact =ForkSpec=s is rewritten from original F
  to clone K, and an existing recursive-content occurrence in the saved viewforest
  may likewise be replaced by its newly visible substitute. The content
  occurrence carries =(inPlaceOf F)=; the root carries nothing, since the
  view is re-rooted at K, and a root marked =(inPlaceOf F)= would be read
  as a perspective's stand-in for F, which the root homes show as itself;
  from the root homes the clone stands in its own home, so shows. The
  refreshed viewforest remains under the same view ID, and the open-view root
  index is recomputed from it, so that buffer is thereafter registered as
  rooted at K. Collateral rerenders do not replace roots;
  their content occurrences substitute as any refresh's do. A later
  independent single-root request for F still opens raw F.

  Moreover, the immediate children of any node shown raw where another
  of its mirrors would stand -- a partner folder member other than a
  forkee as such (a claiming member of a forks folder), or a view root
  the skgrepo restriction keeps its mirror shown from -- are also shown raw
  ('is_raw_where_another_mirror_would_stand'): the user is looking at
  the original, so its children are the original's, not the mirror's.
  This is one level deep -- substitution resumes at the grandchildren --
  and it is strict: the question is asked under no skgrepo restriction,
  so a node whose only other mirror is invisible under the skgrepo
  restriction is shown raw too, and so are its children.

  A substituted viewnode carries the keyed form
  =(inPlaceOf N)= -- crucial =ĥ= immediately after =K= -- naming the original it
  stands for. The part is load-bearing at save: wherever it
  appears, extraction collects N rather than the carrying node's own
  ID, so a container's contains list round-trips to the original
  instead of being rewritten to the mirror. Edits to the shown
  node itself (title, body, folders) still save to the shown node.
  A buffer whose part the server would not have rendered (the headline
  bearing it is no mirror of N, under no skgrepo restriction) aborts
  the save with an explanation naming the mirror the tree's perspective
  would show. Any mirror may bear the part, whichever perspective or
  skgrepo restriction drew it. A
  switch of the skgrepo restriction that makes a
  shown substitute's skgrepo restricted makes it vanish, with its
  subtree (the fact does not survive, and nothing stands where the
  substitute stood), and the refresh draws the original it stood for,
  which disk supplementation preserves in its container's contains. A
  restricted original whose record is visible stands as a restricted
  viewnode, never substituted. When the original itself becomes the
  mirror shown, the refresh reinstates it beside the headline that
  bore the part, which, unwarranted as itself, vanishes.

  A mirror shown in N's place carries a birth fact for the relation
  between them: =(forks out)= when it reaches N by forker steps only
  (it forks a note that ... forks N), =(forks in)= when by
  forkee steps only (N forks a note that ... forks it), =(forks out)=
  where both hold (mutual forks), none otherwise (a sibling, say);
  'side_of_mirror_shown' in =server/dbs/in_rust_graph/mirrors.rs=.
